##
    Shared and public answer different questions.

    A declaration may carry both, and neither one narrows the other, so they are worth
    seeing apart before seeing them together.

        shared   asks how many there are.   One, for everybody, rather than one per instance.
        public   asks who can reach it.     Anyone, rather than only this type.

    A 'shared model' cannot be instantiated and every member of it is shared — there is one
    of each, for the whole program. That says nothing at all about who may reach them. A
    member of a shared model with no visibility written is private, exactly as a member of an
    ordinary model is, and reaching it from outside is refused.

    So the four reaches are the same wherever a member lives:

        (nothing)   the type that declares it
        protected   that type and everything extending it
        internal    anywhere in this project
        public      anywhere at all

    Everything below is one program, so 'internal' and 'public' cannot be told apart by
    running it. samples/library shows that boundary; this shows the rest.
##

##
    @summary: A counter shared by the whole program.

    @remarks: Every member here is shared — there is one of each, and 'new Tally()' is
    refused. What each member says after that is about reach, and the two are independent.
##
shared model Tally

    ##
        @summary: How many times anything has been counted.

        @remarks: No visibility written, so this reaches Tally and nothing else. It is shared
        and private at the same time, which is the whole point: one of it, reachable here.
    ##
    integer counted = 0;

    ##
        @summary: The largest count seen.

        @remarks: 'internal' widens it to the project. In a program of one file that is every
        line of it, so this and 'public' behave alike here — the difference appears the moment
        a second project references this one.
    ##
    internal integer highest = 0;

    # @summary: Counts one more thing, and is reachable from anywhere.
    # @yields: the running total after counting.
    public integer function Count()
        Tally.counted = Tally.counted + 1;

        if Tally.counted > Tally.highest
            Tally.highest = Tally.counted;
        end if

        yield Tally.counted;
    end function

    ##
        @summary: The running total, without adding to it.

        @remarks: Reaching 'counted' from here is fine — a private member is reachable
        throughout the type that declares it, however many functions that is.

        @yields: how many things have been counted.
    ##
    public integer function Counted()
        yield Tally.counted;
    end function

    ##
        @summary: Puts the count back to nothing.

        @remarks: Private, like the field it resets. A shared model may keep things to itself
        exactly as any other model does.
    ##
    function Reset()
        Tally.counted = 0;
    end function

    # @summary: Resets the count from outside, which is what 'public' allows.
    public function Restart()
        Tally.Reset();
    end function

end model

##
    @summary: An ordinary model, for comparing the same four words against.

    @remarks: Nothing here is shared, so each instance carries its own. The visibility words
    mean exactly what they meant above.
##
model Reading

    # @summary: What this reading measured. Private, so only Reading may read it.
    integer value;

    # @summary: Anyone may make one.
    # @measured: what it measured.
    public function Reading(integer measured)
        this.value = measured;
    end function

    ##
        @summary: What it measured.

        @remarks: 'protected' would put this within reach of anything extending Reading and
        nowhere else. It is public instead, because callers ask.

        @yields: the value measured.
    ##
    public integer function Value()
        yield this.value;
    end function

end model

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine("== one tally, shared by everything ==");
        Console.WriteLine("  first count:  " + Tally.Count());
        Console.WriteLine("  second count: " + Tally.Count());
        Console.WriteLine("  and asking:   " + Tally.Counted());

        ##
            Tally.counted is not written here, and cannot be. It is shared — there is one of
            it — and it is private, so only Tally reaches it. Being one of a thing and being
            reachable are separate questions, and 'shared' answers only the first.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== shared says how many, not who ==");
        Console.WriteLine("  Tally.Counted() is public, so this line compiles");
        Console.WriteLine("  Tally.counted is private, so writing it would not");

        ##
            Reset is shared and private; Restart is shared and public. Both are one function
            for the whole program. Only one of them can be called from here.
        ##
        Tally.Restart();
        Console.WriteLine("  after Restart(): " + Tally.Counted());

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== an ordinary model, one per instance ==");

        Reading first = new Reading(7);
        Reading second = new Reading(9);

        Console.WriteLine("  first:  " + first.Value());
        Console.WriteLine("  second: " + second.Value());
        Console.WriteLine("  each carries its own, where Tally carried one for everybody");
    end function

end model
